  "body": "## What happens\n\n- The post refuses a dwell on G86, G73 and G84. The G86 refusal text names the Fanuc and Haas formats. The Mazak EIA/ISO programming manual gives a P word on these cycles.\n- The post writes work offsets 1-6 only (G54-G59). The Mazak manual gives `G54.1 P<n>` additional work offsets.\n- A smoothing request writes only the note \"SMOOTHING NOT WRITTEN - NO MAZAK FORM STATED\". The Mazak manual gives the high-speed and smoothing words (`G61.1`, `G05 P2` / `G05 P0`).\n\n## Expected\n\n- The post writes `P` on G86, G73 and G84 where the Mazak manual permits it. If not, the refusal text gives the correct reason for Mazak.\n- The post writes `G54.1 P<n>` for offsets above 6, and `workOffsetMax` is set to match.\n- The post writes the smoothing words that the Mazak manual gives, with their stated conditions (compensation, mirror and scaling cancelled). If not, it continues to name the request and write nothing.\n\n## Evidence (upstream main `eafe6aec6`)\n\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:943-963`: the dwell refusals (#1445, #2356).\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:21-22`, `:508-518`: `workOffsetMax = 6` and `workOffsetName`.\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:566-579`: `smoothOn()` (#616).\n\nFound by the post audit against the Mazak manuals (#4130).\n\n## Done means\n\nEach item is written with a golden, or stays refused by name with a reason that is correct for Mazak.\n",
